================================================================================
CROSS-n: does ANY (k-1)-speed base + outlier catch the gap (1/(k+1),2/(2k+1))?
================================================================================
  k(=n-1)                gap  #bases  catching bases   min in-gap M
        5         (1/6,2/11)     244               0          EMPTY
        6         (1/7,2/13)     355               0          EMPTY
        7         (1/8,2/15)     408               1           3/23
         e.g. base [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7] + 18 = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 18] -> M=3/23
        8         (1/9,2/17)     424               0          EMPTY
        9        (1/10,2/19)     432               0          EMPTY
       10        (1/11,2/21)     435               0          EMPTY
       11        (1/12,2/23)     440               0          EMPTY
       12        (1/13,2/25)     444               0          EMPTY
       13        (1/14,2/27)     448               1           3/41

  => the CROSSOVER k* = largest k with a catching base.  For k > k*,
     NO single-outlier family lands in the gap (uniform Dx<D).  If k=12
     is EMPTY (> k*), the single-outlier bounded case CLOSES at LRC(14).
     Small k catch (denser ladder j/(kj+r), rung inside); large k skip
     (Farey rungs 1/(k+1),2/(2k+1) bracket the gap).
